spoz2 0.1

# SPOZ2 described in SPOZ2.  This file is the authoritative statement of
# what the SPOZ2 tooling is supposed to do, and it is parsed by the tests.

system spoz2
  SPOZ2 describes what software is supposed to be doing, in a form humans
  and AI can both read.  A description is made of invariants bound to
  version ranges, and it evolves as the software evolves.  It describes
  intended behaviour and constraints, not the current implementation.

invariant history-is-never-deleted
  since 0.1
  When an invariant changes, the old entry stays in the file and remains
  authoritative for the versions it applied to.  Tooling must never drop,
  merge or rewrite historical entries.
  Enforced mechanically by "spoz2 guard": between two revisions of a file,
  entries may be added, an entry may gain an "until" (retirement), and
  prose may be appended — but existing entries are never altered or
  removed.  This sentence was itself added by appending, under guard.

invariant latest-since-governs
  since 0.1
  For a given invariant name and software version V, the entry that
  governs is the one with the greatest "since" not after V, unless that
  entry has an "until" at or before V, in which case nothing governs.

invariant prose-is-authoritative-intent
  since 0.1
  The human-language text of an invariant is its meaning.  The structured
  parts (name, since, until) exist only to give it identity and scope.
  Tooling must not require the prose to be machine executable.

invariant strict-identity-loose-meaning
  since 0.1
  Names and versions are parsed strictly and validated.  Everything else is
  free text.  New structure is added only when real use shows that
  precision there is worth more than flexibility.

invariant future-intent-is-roadmap
  since 0.1
  An entry whose since is ahead of the system's current version is
  stated intent that does not yet govern: the roadmap. Tooling presents
  it (spoz2 roadmap) and agents implement toward it. Completing a
  roadmap item changes the code, never the entry - the entry simply
  begins to govern when its version ships.

invariant tooling-is-agent-invocable
  since 0.2
  The SPOZ2 tooling provides a non-interactive command interface that
  humans, scripts and AI agent harnesses can invoke. Commands intended
  for automation return structured results and distinguish success,
  invalid input and failed operations unambiguously.

invariant core-behaviour-needs-no-ai
  since 0.2
  Parsing, validation, version selection and history protection are
  deterministic operations. They do not require a language model,
  network service or interpretation of invariant prose.
